      "urpx:changeReason": {
        "@id": "urpx:initial"
      },
      "urpx:description": "SMUD Rate Schedule SSR — customer-generation overlay applied on top of the customer's base SMUD rate (R-TOD for residential per Sheet 2.IV.A.6) for renewable-fueled electrical generation facilities and behind-the-meter storage. Effective March 1, 2022; replaces NEM1 for new residential interconnections approved on/after that date (existing NEM1 customers are grandfathered through 2030, modeled separately). Export-compensation semantics: bidirectional metering measures the flow of electricity in both directions; any electricity that is exported to SMUD is credited at the Export Compensation Rate ($0.0740/kWh, effective March 1, 2022) on the customer's bill. The export credit can ONLY offset electricity-usage charges (not the System Infrastructure Fixed Charge, demand charges, or program fees). Any remaining credit carries over to subsequent billing periods. The Export Compensation Rate is updated by the Board every four years starting 2026, bounded by ±30% per cycle, using a combination of publicly available local indices and SMUD actual costs (Sheet 2.VI). Eligibility (Sheet 2.IV.A): facility on customer's premises operating in parallel with SMUD's distribution, meets Rule 21, sized to offset up to 110% of customer's own consumption, single metering point, generating capacity ≤3,000 kW (CEC-AC for photovoltaic; nameplate AC for other types). Storage facilities qualify regardless of date of approval (per Sheet 1 asterisk). Residential SSR customers MUST also be on Rate Schedule R-TOD (Sheet 2.IV.A.6); urpx:applicableToRatePlan names the two R-TOD bases, smud:tod-5-8pm and smud:tod-low-use. Generator standby charges are waived for the portion of customer load served by the SSR-eligible facility (Sheet 1.III). Renewable fuel sources include photovoltaic, wind, biomass, solar thermal, geothermal, fuel cells using renewable fuels, small hydroelectric (with environmental conditions), digester gas, municipal solid waste conversion, landfill gas, ocean wave, ocean thermal, and tidal current — defined per the CEC's Renewable Portfolio Standard Eligibility Guidebook.",
